  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily severe weather threat through the end of \nthe week.\n\nAloft, upper level ridging over the Ohio River Valley slowly\nshifts eastward this afternoon as a shortwave trough pivots\nover the forecast area. At the surface, a strong low pressure\nsystem tracks east across Canada through the end of the week\nwith the associated fronts lingering near the forecast area. On\nFriday, the associated cold front tracks across the forecast\narea. Daily shower and thunderstorm chances continue through \nthe end of the week across the forecast area with a hot and\nhumid airmass yielding ample CAPE (1500-2500 J/kg)each\nafternoon.  \n\nThe forecast for Wednesday through Friday remains uncertain \nregarding timing and thunderstorm coverage. Should thunderstorm \ndevelopment coincide with peak daytime heating, instability will\nyield strong to severe thunderstorms across the forecast area. \nThe best chance for severe weather is on Thursday as the \naforementioned cold front approaches the forecast area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions yield possible record high \n minimum temperatures this week\n\nA hot and humid airmass builds overhead this week with heat\nindices soaring into the upper 90s to low 100s each afternoon.\nUncertainty with max heat index tomorrow due to potential\nconvection and cloudcover. May end up with some advisories. \nHowever, Thursday has the potential to be the warmest day of the\nweek with heat indices forecast to exceed 100 in the valleys \nwest of the Blue Ridge and 105 along the I-95 corridor. Humid \nconditions continue overnight, with near record high minimum \ntemperatures possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Decreasing heat & thunderstorm chances leading \ninto Labor Day weekend. \n\nOn Saturday, high pressure builds at the surface over the Great \nLakes further advancing a cold front south across our region. Drier \nair trailing behind this boundary will stabilize conditions and \nreduce thunderstorm chances through the weekend. Brief showers or \nthunderstorms remain possible as a few disturbances round the base \nof the upper-level trough, but overall, no hazardous weather is \nexpected through the beginning of next week. Through the long \nweekend, day time temperatures continue to drop back into seasonable \nconditions for early September. With dew-points in the low to mid \n60s, seasonable temperatures, and relatively low precipitation \nchances, Labor Day weekend is shaping up to be a pleasant September \nweekend.",
